  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a reversible chemical strategy for modifying an antibody-like protein through cysteine thiols. On the left, the protein is shown with exposed sulfhydryl groups, which react with a maleimide reagent by Michael addition. This forms stable-looking thioether linkages, depicted by two attached maleimide-derived groups on the protein in the central structure.\n\nThe key point is that this conjugation is not permanently fixed. A labeled retro-Michael reaction can occur, releasing the maleimide-related species and regenerating free thiols on the protein. The released maleimide then appears able to transfer to another thiol-containing molecule in serum, shown on the right as HSA, likely human serum albumin. The final purple HSA box bearing the maleimide adduct indicates unwanted exchange from the original protein conjugate to serum proteins.\n\nOverall, the schematic emphasizes the instability or reversibility of maleimide-based thiol conjugation in biological environments. It highlights a sequence of initial attachment, possible deconjugation, and subsequent capture by serum thiols, suggesting a mechanism for loss of the original conjugate and off-target binding in circulation.",
